saitama he goes by many different names  in pop culture bald Cape Cape Baldy the  one punch man but rarely do you hear him  being called hero a common conversation  topic I've heard discussed over the last  number of years is beam is Saitama  really a hero with this question in mind  I decided to take to my Twitter to ask  my followers their opinions on this  controversial character asking do you  think Saitama is heroic with over 3,000  votes cast the results were just as  divided as I expected with the comment  section below demonstrating an even more  conflicted outlook many exclaiming  absolutely not and other screaming yes  of course he is so today I want to take  this as an opportunity to analyze and  breakdown the character and personality  of one of animes most popular modern  protagonists a character that's viewed  by some as lazy and uninspired and by  others inspirational and complex all in  an effort to determine if he is in fact  an heroic figure today I want to analyze  and breakdown one punch man's main  protagonist  [Music]  before we talk about saitama's heroism  or lack thereof I think it's important  we first define what makes for a heroic  act km Weiland author of creating  character arcs defines it as the hero  puts himself out there and gives up  something major and personal often his  own life in order to achieve a goal or  saved someone else  in other words sacrifice personal or  otherwise seems to play an important  role in defining an heroic act  typically this character finds  themselves in a number of familiar  situations most depicting the hero with  his back against the wall fighting for  what he thinks is right regardless of  the dangers associated and seeing as one  punch man deals with superhero tropes  specifically let's take a look at the  film Captain America the First Avenger  acting as the prime example of what I  think most people might agree on is the  definition of aneroid figure in pop  culture Captain America in this film is  certainly not short of his fair share of  heroic moments the most obvious being  its ending as cab makes the difficult  decision to crash the plane he's flying  to save the world choosing to end his  own life for the greater good losing  everything important to him in the  process however I think the best example  of aneroid moment from the film is this  this is a terrific example of a scene  that shows off exactly what many would  argue are important to tributes in  making your hero a hero without delay  casting yourself into harm's way in  order to preserve the well-being of  someone other than yourself an  altruistic act of good a common argument  I've heard is that Saitama a person  that's resilient to the point of being  near indestructible cannot have a scene  like this he can't be brave like Captain  America or spider-man because unlike  them he isn't physically threatened by  anything and therefore can't sacrifice  his physical well-being for the good of  others and that's absolutely true  but it wasn't always the case in one of  the very few glimpses we get as readers  into saitama's past we see a powerless  young disillusioned man apathetic in his  struggle to find a job confronted by a  monster seemingly for the first time in  his life he puts up no fight to protect  his own life once threatened the monster  explains that he is after someone else  and moves along without harming Saitama  on his journey home he encounters the  child that the crab monster is after  before long the crab monster arrives in  the scene to confront the child in front  of Saitama ready to kill him without  thinking Saitama throws himself in  harm's way saving the child's life he  decides in that moment to pursue his  childhood dream of becoming a hero that  defeats villains and he does just that  however in that pursuit he loses the  ability to be physically vulnerable to  attack which is the most common threat  to be issued to a hero by a villain as a  result if the modern Saitama was put  into the same position again it wouldn't  have the same tone or sense of danger  and sacrifice thus losing its air of  heroism and the author of one punch man  was aware of that which is why we have  characters like jenos that exemplify a  more traditional power set and moral  compass dealing with situations for the  good of others even in the face of  absolute destruction however does this  mean that because of Saitama seemingly  invulnerable state of being it's fair to  say that since gaining his powers he  hasn't done anything nor is he capable  of doing anything heroic no in Robert  McKee's book story  he writes story is about archetypes not  stereotypes the archetypal story on  earth a universally human experience  then wraps itself inside a unique  culture specific expression a  stereotypical story reverses this path  it suffers the poverty of both content  and form it can find itself to a narrow  culture specific experience and dresses  in stale nonspecific generalities in  other words it's the characters and  their arcs that should dictate the  scenes that are created not the other  way around  and so because certain genres produce  stories that often portray certain  characters in specific ways when an  author writes their story they might  feel compelled to shoehorn their  characters into specific scenes they  have in mind to create scenes or story  beats that are more familiar to the  viewers or readers or put more simply  forcing tropes in unnatural ways an  example of this misuse of character can  be observed in the film Suicide Squad  we're a group of villains of varying  expertise and ability are thrown into a  situation they wouldn't naturally choose  to be in however the issue with this  story becomes abundantly clear when  characters like Harlequin Killer Croc  and the rest of the team decide to save  the day at the end not because it makes  sense for their characters to come to  that conclusion but because that's what  happens at the end of these kinds of  films it's a classic case of stereotypes  driving the narrative instead of  character let's go if the area's Suicide  Squad failed I think one punch man seems  to have its strengths first show that  deals with supernatural heroes and  monstrous characters on a regular basis  it never loses itself in the stereotypes  and pitfalls of the genre embracing that  side only when it makes sense or if a  joke is being made  midway through the first season a  character by the name of the deep-sea  king is introduced as he wreaks havoc on  a city claiming the lives of many and  taking out numerous impressive heroes  along the way right as the last line of  defense for the citizens of the city is  crushed Moomin writer a regular man  trying to do the right thing having  traveled miles by bicycle arrives to  help in whatever way he can the  characters watching in the background  and all of us watching from our homes  know that he doesn't stand a chance and  in possibly one of the most moving  moments of the series we watch a man  humble himself both physically and  verbally because he knows that giving  everything he has to protect people is  the right thing to do the devastated and  frightened crowd turned to roaring  support and applause as they cheer for  Moomin Ryder barely standing he uses the  support of the people to psych himself  up for one last great  Yoga nice cycle in this scene Saitama  demonstrates that he is an heroic figure  in ways no other character has been  before in the story to help us fully  understand how one punch man's author  one achieves this we must first  understand the term dramatic irony in  Robert McKee's book story he explains in  dramatic irony the audience knows more  than the character's dramatic irony  creates interest primarily through  concern alone eliminating curiosity  about facts and consequences it  encourages the audience to look more  deeply into the motivations and casual  forces at work in the characters lives  in other words when we the audience know  the outcome of a scene or event we can  more closely pay attention to understand  and appreciate the motivations and  emotional nuances of a scene for  instance in the movie get out directed  by Jordan Peele  upon first viewing we as a viewer can  sense that there is something not quite  right about the Family Rose has brought  her boyfriend Chris to meet and so we  spend quite a bit of time trying to  figure out what the movie isn't telling  us once it's revealed to us what Rose  and her family have in store for Chris  the movie quickly and satisfyingly  reaches its action and suspense-filled  conclusion however upon second viewing  as an audience instead of trying to  figure out what the twists are ending  might be we instead can focus on and  understand the decisions the characters  made along the way to get to the  conclusion a terrific example of what  I'm talking about can be observed with  roses objection to the police officers  request at the beginning of the film to  see Chris's identification upon first  viewing it might appear as though she is  standing up for her boyfriend however it  is in fact her working to avoid a paper  trail being created for the authorities  to follow once chris eventually is  brainwashed by her family if you know  how the scene episode or film ends  before the character does then that is  dramatic irony this is why we enjoy a  film or show more or at least  differently on a second viewing instead  of trying to make sense of what's  happening we can instead focus on  understanding empathizing and dread what  the characters are about to experience  in the case of this particular one punch  man scene there are two important  aspects of the story at play one Saitama  kills the deep-sea King before going  into the altercation we as Vee  know that Saitama is jealous of Genesis  adulation and success with the public  Saitama wants to feel appreciated and  actively goes out of his way to seek  recognition and appreciation earlier in  the story however right after Saitama  kills the deep-sea King the scene skips  the future as both jenos and himself are  opening letters they have received the  mail  amid dozens of fan letters janice has  again received Saitama is excited to  find out that he has won - at this point  we have been led to believe that this  will in fact be a fan letter considering  the great service Saitama provided by  protecting the large crowd from the deep  sea king  however moments later we find out that  it reads no one cares about you you  cowardly cheating bastard this now acts  as the lens for which we viewed the  subsequent flashback of events we now  understand the conclusion and so we can  now focus on the motivations and choice  is made to get to this point once the  letter is shown we return back after the  deep-sea Kings to feed as the crowd  erupts with tears of joy for Saitama as  quickly as it began an individual in the  crowd begins rationalizing that if a  ranked sea hero like Saitama was able to  do what the s-class at a class heroes  couldn't and the hero Association must  be a fraud and doesn't deserve the tax  money they received from the public  overhearing all of this Saitama without  skipping a beat begins gloating and  thanking all of the heroes for their  hard work in softening the villain up  for him he exclaims that now he can  continue to take credit for their hard  work  Saitama in this moment chose to deny  himself the appreciation and respect he  deeply desires and deserves in favor of  preserving an association that refuses  to acknowledge him but one that the  people need to protect them from the  creatures that lurk in wait heroism is  the throwing a sign of things that you  care about in favor of doing the right  thing for others sacrifice as common as  it is in superhero stories usually  manifests itself in the hero putting  himself into life-threatening danger  struggling against a great evil  achieving heroic feats using their  spectacular powers however somewhat  ironically Saitama a hero with power  beyond comprehension manages to become a  hero in a way all of us watching have  the power to be there are many other  instances in the story where Saitama  provides selfless service to those that  do not appreciate him and to those that  do to ask for his help but a hero is not  limited by what is asked of him but what  he asks of himself one punch man reminds  us that sometimes being a  can be as simple as choosing to say a  difficult thing for the right reasons  for a character with abilities as  fantastical and overstated as sigh  Thomas it's ironic to see that his  heroism is not only understated but when  compared to the rest of the heroes that  inhabit the pages of shounen jump and  beyond is both minimized and  unconventional it's made abundantly  clear in the source material that  Saitama is at everyman traditionally  unintelligent and somewhat lazy but on  the rare occasions that he is faced with  issues his powers do not equipment for  he proves time and time again that not  only is he a remarkable character but  also an heroic one thanks for watching  [Music]
 

